#### 6.4.1 Pacing and the cost of idle time

{{if:pacing.old}}*These pacing runs are from the first study ({{pacing.source|s}}, branch
`registrar-overhead-oct27`); they were not repeated on the new code. Pacing concerns the
harness's idle strategy, which the registrar changes do not affect; the per-call
costs in them are those of the old registrar.*
{{endif}}
Earlier realtime results reported registration latencies of tens of microseconds,
against sub-microsecond costs in the microbenchmarks. Before the realtime sweep, a
pacing check separated the possible causes. It ran two load points, each 15 min of real
time after a 6 h simulated warm-up (hybrid clock, Poisson arrivals, C = 1000, seed 1),
with each of two idle strategies between arrivals:

* `sleep`: the worker blocks on a timer;
* `spin`: the worker busy-waits.

{{table:pacing}}

Median latency by the idle time that preceded the call:

{{table:pacing_idle}}

For reference, simulated runs, which execute operations back to back, give medians of
{{simlat.C1000-fresh5.registration.p50_us|f:2}} µs (registration) and
{{simlat.C1000-fresh5.query.p50_us|f:2}} µs (query) at 5 fresh attempts/s, and
{{simlat.C1000-fresh100.registration.p50_us|f:2}} µs and {{simlat.C1000-fresh100.query.p50_us|f:2}} µs at
100 fresh attempts/s.

Interpretation:

1. **Sleep pacing distorts both queueing and service time.** With `sleep`, the worker
   wakes late: the median queue delay is
   {{pacing.C1000-fresh5-query20-sleep.queue_delay_p50_us|f:0}} µs at low load and
   {{pacing.C1000-fresh100-query1000-sleep.queue_delay_p50_us|f:0}} µs at high load. With `spin`
   it is {{pacing.C1000-fresh5-query20-spin.queue_delay_p50_us|f:2}} µs and
   {{pacing.C1000-fresh100-query1000-spin.queue_delay_p50_us|f:2}} µs. Service times are also
   inflated after a sleep: at high load the median registration takes
   {{pacing.C1000-fresh100-query1000-sleep.registration.p50_us|f:2}} µs with `sleep` and
   {{pacing.C1000-fresh100-query1000-spin.registration.p50_us|f:2}} µs with `spin`. The likely cause
   is CPU power management: a blocked core lowers its clock or enters an idle state.
   macOS does not expose the core state, so this cannot be confirmed directly.
2. **With `spin`, high-load latency matches the simulated and microbenchmark costs.**
   The remaining dependence on idle time is visible only at low load:
   registration rises from {{pacing.C1000-fresh5-query20-spin.registration.p50_us|f:2}} µs (median)
   at {{pacing.C1000-fresh5-query20-spin.requests_per_s|f:0}} requests/s to {{pacing.C1000-fresh100-query1000-spin.registration.p50_us|f:2}} µs at
   {{pacing.C1000-fresh100-query1000-spin.requests_per_s|f:0}} requests/s. This is consistent
   with a call after a long gap finding the registrar's data out of the CPU caches.
   It is a property of a mostly idle registrar, not a capacity limit.
3. **Consequences for the realtime measurements:**
   * the realtime sweep uses `spin` pacing;
   * service-time distributions are reported per load level, not as one number;
   * throughput limits come from latency and queueing under load. Process CPU time is
     meaningless under `spin`, where the worker is always busy.
   * The earlier realtime bundles combined sleep pacing, Rosetta translation and
     non-stationary measurement windows, so their latencies are not comparable.

#### 6.4.2 Throughput and saturation

{{if:table:realtime}}The realtime block ran each point with the hybrid clock (a simulated warm-up of
3 h to reach the equilibrium state, then wall-clock measurement), spin pacing,
Poisson arrivals and no per-operation records, on an otherwise idle machine. A monitor
recorded the load average, the busiest processes and the thermal state every minute;
points whose window overlapped foreign load (load average above 3, or another process
above 30% CPU — the machine runs endpoint-security software) or a thermal limit are
marked in the last column ({{rt.contaminated_points|f:0}} of {{rt.points|f:0}} points).

{{table:realtime}}

**CPU utilisation.** Under spin pacing the worker thread never blocks, so the
process uses one core all the time and process CPU time says nothing about load
(§6.4.1). "Registrar busy" is therefore the time spent inside `TopicTable` calls
divided by wall time: (registrations/s × mean ns per registration + queries/s × mean
ns per query) / 10⁹. It is the registrar core's share of one core; the harness's own
work (arrival generation, bookkeeping) is excluded.

{{if:figure:realtime_throughput}}{{figref:realtime_throughput}} plots throughput, busy fraction and queueing against the offered rate.

{{fig:realtime_throughput}}Served requests/s (top), registrar-core busy fraction of one core (middle) and queue delay p50/p99
(bottom) against offered requests/s: the query sweep at C = 1000 and the two points at C = 5000 (hybrid clock, 3 h
simulated warm-up, 10 min wall-clock windows, 100 fresh attempts/s, Poisson arrivals, one seed). {{if:rt.C1000.sat_offered_query}}C = 1000
saturates at {{rt.C1000.sat_offered_query|sig:2}} queries/s{{else}}C = 1000 is served in full up to the highest rate offered{{endif}}{{if:rt.C5000.sat_offered_query}}; C = 5000
saturates at {{rt.C5000.sat_offered_query|sig:2}} queries/s, where the queue delay jumps to seconds{{endif}}.{{endfig}}
{{endif}}
{{if:rt.knee_offered_query}}**Saturation, per capacity.** A point counts as served in full when at least 98% of the
offered queries are served and the queue-delay p99 is below 10 ms.

* **C = 1000:** {{if:rt.C1000.sat_offered_query}}the knee lies between {{rt.C1000.served_offered_query|sig:2}} and
  {{rt.C1000.sat_offered_query|sig:2}} offered queries/s; at the latter the process served {{rt.C1000.sat_served_rps|f:0}} requests/s with the
  registrar core busy {{rt.C1000.sat_busy|pct:1}}.{{else}}knee not reached. Every offered rate up to {{rt.C1000.max_offered_query|sig:2}} queries/s
  plus 100 fresh attempts/s was served in full, so the knee is at or above {{rt.knee_requests_rps|sig:3}} requests/s.
  At that rate the registrar core was busy {{rt.knee_busy|pct:1}} of the time and the p99 queue delay was
  {{rt.knee_qd_p99_us|f:1}} µs.{{endif}}
* **C = 5000:** {{if:rt.C5000.sat_offered_query}}knee reached at or below {{rt.C5000.sat_offered_query|sig:2}} offered queries/s.
  {{rt.C5000.served_offered_query|sig:2}} queries/s was served in full; at {{rt.C5000.sat_offered_query|sig:2}} queries/s the process served only
  {{rt.C5000.sat_served_rps|f:0}} of {{rt.C5000.sat_offered_rps|f:0}} offered requests/s, with the registrar core busy
  {{rt.C5000.sat_busy|pct:1}} and a median queue delay of {{rt.C5000.sat_qd_p50_s|f:1}} s (the backlog grew through the window).
  Registrations wait in the same queue, so occupancy fell to {{rt.C5000.sat_utilisation|pct:1}} of C.
  The cause is the longer topic lists: with five times the capacity each topic holds about five times as many ads, and a
  lookup visits every ad of its topic (§6.1). The mean `TOPICQUERY` cost was {{rt.C5000.sat_q_mean_ns|f:0}} ns against
  {{rt.C1000.top_q_mean_ns|f:0}} ns at C = 1000 at the same offered rate. The worker thread saturated before the
  registrar core reached 100%; the rest of its time is harness work (arrival generation, clock reads, bookkeeping).{{else}}no
  offered rate saturated the worker ({{rt.C5000.points|f:0}} points, up to {{rt.C5000.max_offered_query|sig:2}} queries/s).{{endif}}{{else}}*No query-sweep point was served in full, or the sweep did not run; no knee is reported.*{{endif}}

{{if:rt.validation.utilisation_ratio}}**Hybrid clock validation.** A fully realtime run (45 min real warm-up, so its
occupancy may still be rising) with the same configuration as a hybrid point gave an occupancy ratio (realtime/hybrid) of
{{rt.validation.utilisation_ratio|f:3}}, a mean query cost ratio of {{rt.validation.q_mean_ns_ratio|f:2}} and a mean
registration cost ratio of {{rt.validation.r_mean_ns_ratio|f:2}}.{{else}}*Provisional: the fully realtime validation point did not run; the hybrid clock is
validated only by `TestHybridTableEquivalence`.*{{endif}}

{{if:table:pprof_cpu}}**CPU profile** at the highest C = 1000 rate, 640,000 queries/s (top functions by flat time, measurement phase only):

{{table:pprof_cpu}}

*Caveat.* The profile is dominated by overhead outside the registrar logic, so it shows
that the registrar core is not the bottleneck rather than how registrar time divides.
Span clearing (`runtime.memclrNoHeapPointers`, {{rt.pprof.memclr_flat|pct:0}} flat) is reached through the
allocator from the 16-entry result slice that `RandomNodes` allocates per lookup; it
does not appear in the timed per-call cost ({{rt.knee_requests_rps|sig:3}} requests/s at
{{rt.max_busy|pct:1}} busy), so it is a runtime/allocator effect under the harness's heap, not
lookup work. Clock reads (`runtime.nanotime`, {{rt.pprof.nanotime_cum|pct:0}} cumulative) come almost entirely
from the harness's hybrid clock (`hybridClock.Now`, {{rt.pprof.hybridclock_cum|pct:0}}).
{{else}}*No CPU profile was recorded.*{{endif}}
{{if:rt.reg.flagged}}
*Caveat.* {{rt.reg.flagged|f:0}} of the {{rt.reg.points|f:0}} registration-heavy points (b) overlapped background
load (see the contamination column), so their queue-delay tails (p99 {{rt.reg.qd_p99_min_us|f:0}}–{{rt.reg.qd_p99_max_us|f:0}} µs)
are provisional.
{{endif}}
{{else}}> **Provisional — realtime validation pending.** The realtime block produced no
> admissible point. The estimates below come from per-call costs only.{{endif}}

**Per-call costs under the other instruments.** For comparison, the request rate at
which one core would be fully busy with registrar-core work alone, at the same request
mix, from the simulated runs and the pacing check:

{{table:capacity_provisional}}

From the microbenchmarks alone (§6.1), one core executes about
{{cap.micro.RegisterFresh/N=1000/T=16.ops_per_s|sig:2}} fresh registrations/s (N = 1000, T = 16),
{{cap.micro.RandomNodes/N=1000/T=16.ops_per_s|sig:2}} lookups/s at T = 16 and
{{cap.micro.RandomNodes/N=5000/T=64.ops_per_s|sig:2}} lookups/s at T = 64.
{{if:share.cgo.total_ns}}A full `REGTOPIC` costs about {{share.cgo.total_ns|sig:2}} ns once validation is included (§6.1),
so a core spent only on `REGTOPIC` handling would serve about
{{share.cgo.regtopic_per_core|sig:2}} requests/s; validation, not the registrar core, sets that
limit.{{endif}}
